B2B Order Management Platform
Software Requirements Specification (SRS)

Project Type: Full Stack Web Application
Domain: Full Stack Development
Development Mode: Individual Project
Duration: 1 Month
Frontend: React.js
Backend: Node.js + Express.js
Database: PostgreSQL
Authentication: JWT
API Architecture: REST API

1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose

The purpose of this document is to define the functional and non-functional requirements of the B2B Order Management Platform.

The system is designed as a full-stack web application that provides a centralized platform for managing products, inventory, shopping carts, customer orders, user authentication, administrative activities, and order-related records.

This document establishes the project scope, system requirements, user roles, business requirements, technical expectations, assumptions, constraints, and acceptance requirements.

The document will be used as a reference throughout the development, testing, validation, documentation, and final review stages of the project.

1.2 Project Overview

The B2B Order Management Platform is a web-based application designed to simulate a business-to-business product ordering workflow.

The platform allows business customers to:

Create an account.
Log in securely.
Browse available products.
View product information.
Add products to a shopping cart.
Modify cart quantities.
Place orders.
View order history.
Track order information.
Access order invoices.

Administrators can:

Manage products.
Manage inventory.
Monitor customer orders.
Update order status.
Manage product stock.
Monitor important system activities.
Review audit records.

The application follows a full-stack architecture in which the React frontend communicates with the Node.js and Express.js backend through REST APIs. PostgreSQL is used for persistent data storage.

2. Problem Statement

Many small and medium-sized businesses may manage B2B ordering processes using manual communication, spreadsheets, phone calls, messaging applications, or disconnected systems.

Such processes can create several operational problems:

Product information may not be centralized.
Inventory information may become outdated.
Orders may be difficult to track.
Manual order processing can introduce errors.
Customer order history may be difficult to maintain.
Administrative activities may not have a proper activity record.
User access may not be properly controlled.
Business owners may not have a centralized view of order operations.

The proposed system addresses these problems by providing a centralized digital platform for product, cart, order, inventory, authentication, and activity management.

3. Project Objectives

The primary objectives of the project are:

To develop a functional full-stack B2B order management platform.
To provide a centralized product and ordering workflow.
To implement secure user registration and authentication.
To implement JWT-based authentication.
To implement role-based access control.
To provide product management functionality for administrators.
To provide product browsing functionality for customers.
To implement shopping cart management.
To implement order creation and order tracking.
To maintain accurate inventory information.
To automatically update inventory after successful orders.
To develop REST APIs for frontend-backend communication.
To store application data in PostgreSQL.
To implement input validation.
To implement structured error handling.
To maintain audit-friendly activity records.
To generate order invoices.
To test and validate core application functionality.
To maintain professional project documentation.
To produce a portfolio-ready full-stack project.

4. Stakeholders

The major stakeholders of the system are:

Stakeholder	Description	Main Interest
Customer	Business user who purchases products	Product browsing, cart, orders and invoices
Administrator	Authorized user responsible for system management	Products, inventory, orders and activities
Business Owner	Owner or manager of the business	Overall business ordering workflow
Developer	Person responsible for developing the system	Implementation, testing and maintenance
Mentor / Reviewer	Project reviewer	Requirements, quality, evidence and documentation

5. User Roles
5.1 Customer

A customer is an authenticated business user who can interact with the ordering system.

The customer shall be able to:

Register an account.
Log in.
View available products.
Search or browse products.
View product details.
Add products to the cart.
Update product quantities.
Remove products from the cart.
View cart totals.
Place orders.
View order history.
View individual order details.
Track order status.
View or download invoices.

5.2 Administrator

An administrator is an authorized user who manages the operational aspects of the platform.

The administrator shall be able to:

Log in securely.
Access the administration panel.
Create products.
View products.
Update products.
Delete products.
Update inventory quantities.
View customer orders.
Update order status.
Monitor important activities.
Review audit records.

6. Functional Requirements
FR-01: User Registration

The system shall provide a registration interface for new customers.

The registration process shall collect required information such as:

Full name
Company name
Email address
Phone number
Password
Password confirmation

The system shall validate required fields before creating an account.

The system shall reject registration when:

Required information is missing.
Email format is invalid.
Password confirmation does not match.
The email address is already registered.

Passwords shall be securely hashed before being stored in the database.

FR-02: User Login

The system shall allow registered users to authenticate using their credentials.

The login process shall:

Accept valid user credentials.
Verify the credentials.
Generate a JWT token after successful authentication.
Return the authentication response to the frontend.
Allow access to protected resources according to the user's role.

Invalid credentials shall return an appropriate error response.

FR-03: JWT Authentication

The system shall use JSON Web Tokens for authentication.

JWT authentication shall be used to:

Identify authenticated users.
Protect private API endpoints.
Maintain authenticated sessions.
Support role-based authorization.

The backend shall validate JWT tokens before allowing access to protected resources.


FR-04: Role-Based Access Control

The system shall support at least two roles:

Customer
Administrator

Customers shall only have access to customer-level functionality.

Administrators shall have access to administrative functionality.

Unauthorized users shall not be allowed to access protected administrative endpoints.


7. Product Requirements
FR-05: Product Creation

Administrators shall be able to create new products.

Product information may include:

Product name
Product description
Category
Price
Stock quantity
Product image
Product status

The system shall validate product information before storing it.


FR-06: Product Viewing

Customers shall be able to view available products.

The product listing shall display relevant information such as:

Product name
Description
Price
Availability
Stock status
Product image


FR-07: Product Update

Administrators shall be able to update existing product information.

Changes may include:

Product name
Description
Price
Category
Stock quantity
Product status


FR-08: Product Deletion

Administrators shall be able to remove products from the product catalogue.

The system shall ensure that product deletion does not create invalid database relationships.



8. Inventory Requirements
FR-09: Inventory Management

The system shall maintain the available stock quantity of each product.

Administrators shall be able to update inventory information.

The system shall display appropriate stock availability to customers.


FR-10: Stock Validation

Before an order is created, the system shall verify whether sufficient stock is available.

If the requested quantity is greater than the available quantity, the system shall reject the order and return an appropriate error message.


FR-11: Stock Update

After a successful order is created, the system shall decrease the corresponding product stock quantity.

The stock update and order creation process should maintain data consistency.


9. Cart Requirements
FR-12: Add Product to Cart

Authenticated customers shall be able to add available products to their cart.

The system shall validate:

Product existence.
Product availability.
Requested quantity.
Available stock.

FR-13: Update Cart

Customers shall be able to modify the quantity of products in their cart.

The system shall ensure that the updated quantity does not exceed available stock.

FR-14: Remove Cart Item

Customers shall be able to remove individual products from their cart.

FR-15: Cart Total

The system shall calculate the total value of the cart based on:

Product Price × Quantity

The cart total shall be updated when quantities are changed or items are removed.

10. Order Requirements
FR-16: Order Creation

Authenticated customers shall be able to place orders using items available in their cart.

The order process shall:

Validate the customer's authentication.
Validate cart contents.
Verify product availability.
Verify stock quantities.
Calculate the order total.
Create the order.
Create corresponding order items.
Update product stock.
Record the relevant activity.
Return the order confirmation.
FR-17: Order History

Customers shall be able to view their previous orders.

The order history shall provide information such as:

Order ID
Order date
Total amount
Order status
Number of items
FR-18: Order Details

Customers shall be able to view detailed information about an individual order.

Order details may include:

Order ID
Order date
Customer information
Products
Quantities
Product prices
Total amount
Order status
FR-19: Order Status

The system shall maintain an order status.

Example statuses may include:

Pending
Confirmed
Processing
Shipped
Delivered
Cancelled

Administrators shall be able to update order status according to the business workflow.

11. Invoice Requirements
FR-20: Invoice Generation

The system shall generate an invoice for successfully processed orders.

The invoice shall contain relevant information such as:

Invoice number
Order ID
Order date
Customer details
Product details
Quantity
Unit price
Total amount
Order status

The invoice shall be available to the customer for viewing or downloading.

12. Audit Activity Requirements
FR-21: Audit Activity Records

The system shall maintain records of important system activities.

Examples include:

Activity	Actor
User Registration	Customer
User Login	Customer / Administrator
Product Created	Administrator
Product Updated	Administrator
Product Deleted	Administrator
Order Placed	Customer
Order Status Updated	Administrator
Stock Updated	Administrator

Each audit record should contain information such as:

Activity ID
User ID
Activity type
Description
Timestamp
Relevant entity ID where applicable

Audit records shall help provide traceability for important system operations.

13. Validation Requirements
FR-22: Input Validation

The system shall validate input data on both frontend and backend where appropriate.

Validation shall cover:

Required fields.
Email format.
Password requirements.
Password confirmation.
Phone number format.
Product price.
Product quantity.
Stock quantity.
Order quantity.

Backend validation shall not rely only on frontend validation.

14. Error Handling Requirements
FR-23: Error Handling

The system shall provide structured error handling.

The application shall handle:

Invalid credentials.
Unauthorized access.
Forbidden access.
Invalid input.
Missing resources.
Insufficient stock.
Duplicate registration.
Database errors.
Server errors.
Invalid API requests.

The API shall return appropriate HTTP status codes and meaningful error messages.

15. REST API Requirements
FR-24: REST API

The backend shall expose REST APIs for communication with the React frontend.

The API structure shall be organized into appropriate routes such as:

/api/auth
/api/users
/api/products
/api/cart
/api/orders
/api/inventory
/api/admin
/api/audit-logs

The backend shall use appropriate HTTP methods including:

GET
POST
PUT
PATCH
DELETE

API responses shall use JSON format.

16. Database Requirements
FR-25: PostgreSQL Database

The system shall use PostgreSQL as the primary relational database.

The database shall maintain structured and related records for:

Users
Products
Cart
Cart Items
Orders
Order Items
Audit Logs
FR-26: Database Relationships

The database shall maintain appropriate relationships between entities.

Examples:

User → Cart
User → Orders
Cart → Cart Items
Product → Cart Items
Order → Order Items
Product → Order Items
User → Audit Logs

Foreign keys and appropriate database constraints shall be used to maintain data integrity.

17. Database Migration Requirements
FR-27: Database Migrations

Database schema changes shall be managed through migration files.

Migrations shall allow the project database to be:

Created consistently.
Updated in a controlled manner.
Reproduced by another developer.
Reviewed as part of the project source code.
18. Frontend Requirements
FR-28: React Frontend

The frontend shall be implemented using React.

The frontend shall provide pages/interfaces for:

Login
Registration
Dashboard
Products
Cart
Orders
Admin Panel

The frontend shall communicate with backend services through REST APIs.

FR-29: Loading States

The frontend shall display appropriate loading indicators while data is being retrieved or processed.

FR-30: Empty States

The frontend shall provide meaningful messages when there is no available data.

Examples:

Empty cart.
No orders.
No products.
No search results.
19. Security Requirements
NFR-01: Password Security

User passwords shall be stored using secure password hashing.

Plain-text passwords shall never be stored in the database.

NFR-02: Authentication Security

Protected resources shall require valid authentication.

JWT tokens shall be validated on the backend.

NFR-03: Authorization

Users shall only access functionality permitted by their assigned role.

NFR-04: Environment Variables

Sensitive configuration values such as:

Database credentials.
JWT secret.
API configuration.

shall be stored using environment variables rather than hard-coded in source code.

20. Non-Functional Requirements
NFR-05: Performance

The application should provide reasonable response times under normal expected usage.

Database queries should be structured efficiently.

NFR-06: Reliability

The application should handle expected errors without crashing.

NFR-07: Maintainability

The backend should follow a modular project structure.

Example:

server/
├── controllers/
├── routes/
├── models/
├── middleware/
├── config/
├── migrations/
└── app.js
NFR-08: Scalability

The system should be designed so that additional features can be added without major restructuring.

NFR-09: Usability

The user interface should be simple, consistent, responsive, and easy to navigate.

NFR-10: Data Integrity

The system shall maintain accurate and consistent data through:

Database constraints.
Foreign keys.
Validation.
Transaction handling where required.
21. Project Scope
21.1 In Scope

The project includes:

React frontend.
Node.js backend.
Express.js REST API.
PostgreSQL database.
User registration.
User login.
JWT authentication.
Role-based authorization.
Product management.
Product browsing.
Inventory management.
Cart management.
Order management.
Order status management.
Stock validation.
Stock updates.
Audit activity records.
Invoice generation.
Input validation.
Error handling.
Loading and empty states.
Database migrations.
Testing.
Documentation.
GitHub repository.
Final presentation.

Optional Enhancement:

Docker configuration.
22. Out of Scope

The following functionality is not included in the initial project scope:

Real payment gateway integration.
Real banking transactions.
Real customer financial information.
Integration with external ERP systems.
Integration with real distributor systems.
Unauthorized access to external systems.
Production-level enterprise infrastructure.
Real-world customer data without authorization.
23. Assumptions

The following assumptions apply:

Users have access to a modern web browser.
Customers provide valid registration information.
Administrators are assigned appropriate permissions.
Product information is maintained by authorized administrators.
Product stock information is maintained by the system.
PostgreSQL is available during application execution.
The backend server is available to the React frontend.
Development data is simulated, public, or properly authorized.
External resources used during research will be appropriately cited.
The project will be developed and tested within the internship period.
24. Constraints

The project has the following constraints:

The internship duration is limited to four weeks.
The project is developed individually.
Development must follow the defined project requirements.
Only authorized, public, or simulated data may be used.
Real systems must not be tested without explicit authorization.
The project must maintain organized documentation.
The project must provide evidence of testing and validation.
The final project must be reproducible through the provided documentation.
25. Success Metrics

The following measurable outcomes will be used to evaluate the project:

Metric	Target
Core frontend modules	All planned modules implemented
Authentication	Registration and login functional
JWT Authentication	Successfully implemented
Role-based access	Customer and Admin permissions enforced
Product APIs	CRUD operations functional
Cart	Add, update and remove operations functional
Orders	Order creation and retrieval functional
Inventory	Stock validation and update functional
Audit Logs	Important activities recorded
Validation	Frontend and backend validation implemented
Error Handling	Common API errors handled
Database	PostgreSQL integrated successfully
Testing	Core workflows tested with evidence
Documentation	Complete and reproducible
Presentation	Final project demonstrated
26. Acceptance Criteria

The project shall meet the following acceptance criteria before final submission.

Authentication
A new customer can register successfully.
Existing users can log in.
Invalid credentials are rejected.
Passwords are securely stored.
JWT authentication works correctly.
Authorization
Customers cannot access administrative operations.
Administrators can access authorized administrative functionality.
Products
Administrators can create products.
Administrators can update products.
Administrators can delete products.
Customers can view available products.
Cart
Customers can add products.
Customers can modify quantities.
Customers can remove products.
Cart totals are calculated correctly.
Orders
Customers can place valid orders.
Orders contain the correct products and quantities.
Insufficient stock prevents invalid orders.
Customers can view their order history.
Administrators can manage order status.
Inventory
Stock quantities are maintained.
Stock is validated before order creation.
Stock is updated after successful orders.
Audit
Important activities are recorded.
Audit records contain timestamps and relevant user information.
Validation and Errors
Required fields are validated.
Invalid requests are rejected.
Appropriate error messages are displayed.
API responses use suitable HTTP status codes.
Documentation and Evidence
README is available.
Project setup instructions are documented.
API functionality is documented.
Testing evidence is maintained.
Work logs are maintained.
Important design decisions are documented.
